pkg base v1
  desc "VEIL core primitives — always implicitly available"
  author "VEIL"

  # Review presentation (PR Wizard) — host resolves named strategies.
  # strategy structural | component_sandbox | … ; secondary file_diff; impact dependents
  review
    strategy structural
    fallback structural
    secondary file_diff
    impact dependents

  construct struct
    mt primitive
    desc "Product type — named collection of typed fields"
    visual
      icon "📋"
      color "#14b8a6"
      label "Struct"
    in any

  construct enum
    mt primitive
    desc "Sum type — named set of variants"
    visual
      icon "🔀"
      color "#8b5cf6"
      label "Enum"
    in any

  construct fn
    mt primitive
    desc "Function — callable with parameters and return type"
    visual
      icon "⚡"
      color "#f97316"
      label "Function"
    in any

  construct trait
    mt primitive
    desc "Contract — set of method signatures"
    visual
      icon "🔌"
      color "#10b981"
      label "Trait"
    in any

  construct impl
    mt primitive
    desc "Implementation of a trait for a struct"
    visual
      icon "🔗"
      color "#a855f7"
      label "Implementation"
    in any

  construct mod
    mt primitive
    desc "Module — namespace boundary"
    visual
      icon "📦"
      color "#8b5cf6"
      label "Module"
    in none

  construct group
    mt primitive
    desc "Visual grouping — organizational container (no codegen impact)"
    visual
      icon "📂"
      color "#475569"
      label "Group"
    in mod

  # ─── Universal Semantic Modifiers ─────────────────────────────────────────
  # These annotations are available to ALL projects regardless of paradigm.
  # DDD layers auto-apply them via constraints; non-DDD projects use them
  # directly with @immutable or @equality_by_value on any struct-shaped type.

  construct ImmutableMarker
    kw immutable
    mt struct
    desc "Marker: struct cannot be mutated after creation (no &mut self methods)"
    ann
      immutable: "Value cannot change after creation" flag role:immutable
    visual
      icon "🔒"
      color "#64748b"
      label "Immutable"

  construct EqualityByValueMarker
    kw equality_by_value
    mt struct
    desc "Marker: equality determined by field contents, not identity/reference"
    ann
      equality_by_value: "Equality by field contents" flag role:equality_by_value
    visual
      icon "⚖️"
      color "#64748b"
      label "Value Equality"

  prompt
    You are writing VEIL code using the base layer primitives.

    ## Overview
    The base layer provides the core language primitives that all other layers build
    upon. You rarely use these directly — domain layers (ddd, functional, etc.)
    provide higher-level vocabulary. Use base constructs only when no domain layer
    provides a suitable abstraction.

    ## Constructs
    - `struct` — product type with named fields
    - `enum` — sum type with variants (optionally data-carrying)
    - `fn` — function with parameters, return type, and body
    - `trait` — interface / contract with method signatures
    - `impl` — implementation of a trait for a struct
    - `mod` — module / namespace boundary
    - `group` — visual/organizational container (no codegen effect)

    ## Universal Semantic Modifiers
    Available to ALL projects regardless of paradigm:
    - `@immutable` — struct cannot be mutated after creation. Rust: no `&mut self`
      methods. TypeScript: all fields emit as `readonly`.
    - `@equality_by_value` — equality is by field contents, not identity. Rust:
      Eq + Hash derives. TypeScript: generates `TypeNameEquals(a, b)` helper.

    DDD layers auto-apply these via constraints (ValueObject gets equality_by_value,
    Event gets immutable). Non-DDD projects use them directly on any struct.

    ## Constraints
    - You SHOULD prefer domain-layer constructs over raw base primitives because
      they carry semantic meaning that enables better codegen and visualization.
    - You MUST NOT use `mod` directly when a domain layer provides a container
      (e.g., use `ctx` from ddd.layer instead of raw `mod`).
    - You MUST place constructs inside appropriate containers — `group` for
      organization, `mod` for namespace boundaries.

    ## Common Mistakes
    - Wrong: Using `struct` when modeling a DDD aggregate (use `agg` instead).
    - Wrong: Using `fn` for a service handler (use `handler` or `svc` from ddd.layer).
    - Right: Use base primitives for utility types, pure helpers, or when no
      domain layer applies.
